Use a keycode Chromium recognises for the scratch keymap

A keysym that the active xkb layout group cannot produce is delivered on a
throwaway keycode carrying a one-key scratch keymap, and both places that do
this pick 247 (KEY_RFKILL). That code has no entry in Chromium's evdev ->
DomCode table (ui/events/keycodes/dom/dom_code_data.inc), and
WaylandKeyboard::DispatchKey does

    DomCode dom_code = KeycodeConverter::EvdevCodeToDomCode(key);
    if (dom_code == ui::DomCode::NONE)
      return;

so every Chromium and Electron application -- Chrome, VS Code, Slack, Discord,
Signal -- silently drops the key, both for the RemoteDesktop portal and for the
virtual keyboard. On a us,gr layout that means a dictation tool can type Latin
text into a browser but not Greek; the same happens to any symbol outside the
active group, EuroSign included.

The character itself comes from the keysym rather than from the DomCode
(XkbKeyboardLayoutEngine::Lookup ends in DomKey::FromCharacter(character)), so
any keycode Chromium knows produces the right result. KEY_F24 is in its table,
is not part of the pc+us include the scratch keymap builds on, and is rarely
bound as a shortcut.

Verified on Plasma 6.7.4: wev shows `key: 255 sym: U03B3 utf8: 'γ'` arriving
correctly, while the same keystroke into Chrome 151 produces no keydown event at
all. Qt and GTK clients are unaffected because they resolve the keycode through
xkbcommon directly rather than through a fixed table.

